WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:13.994
A machine with an empty disk and no ISO installs Debian by itself, over the network. The PXE lab: a server that is every piece of a network install, and a diskless client.

00:00:14.094 --> 00:00:25.026
On the map, the two machines share a private segment. The server answers DHCP, TFTP and HTTP there, and nowhere else.

00:00:25.126 --> 00:00:34.899
The lens on the client's cable opens the packet inspector. The filter keeps the first steps of a network boot: DHCP and TFTP.

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:54.941
From the host, wipe the client and install it again over the network.

00:00:55.041 --> 00:01:11.179
The client's network card boots iPXE, and DHCP appears on the cable, read from the bottom: discover, broadcast by the client; offer, from the server; request; acknowledge. Pause freezes the list.

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:22.168
On the client's console: pxelinux came over TFTP with the lab's menu, and its default entry loads the Debian installer over HTTP.

00:01:22.268 --> 00:01:44.133
The installer talks on the serial console, which the host's terminal shows. It fetches its answers from the server, and packages from the Debian mirror through the server's NAT. Nobody types anything.

00:01:44.233 --> 00:02:03.497
Meanwhile on the server: dnsmasq logged the four DHCP messages, discover, offer, request, acknowledge, and every file it sent over TFTP.

00:02:03.597 --> 00:02:14.614
The lease it handed out, and nginx's log: the kernel, the initrd and the answers, over HTTP.

00:02:14.714 --> 00:02:28.700
The installer powers the machine off, and it boots its own disk. Debian 13, installed from nothing but the network.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:39.021 line:6%
Seven exercises and six tests come with the lab, in English and Italian, from its card.
